1
00:00:04,950 --> 00:00:06,880
Oh, boy.

2
00:00:08,580 --> 00:00:09,580
What?

3
00:00:09,580 --> 00:00:13,680
I can't comment without violating our agreement that i'll not criticize your work.

4
00:00:14,650 --> 00:00:16,410
Then what was "Oh, boy"?

5
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:19,710
Great restraint on my part.

6
00:00:19,780 --> 00:00:22,310
There's nothing wrong with the science here.

7
00:00:22,380 --> 00:00:24,750
Perhaps you mean a different thing than I do

8
00:00:24,810 --> 00:00:27,080
when you say, "Science."

9
00:00:33,480 --> 00:00:35,150
Okay, how's that?

10
00:00:35,210 --> 00:00:37,910
You actually had it right in the first place.

11
00:00:38,910 --> 00:00:43,710
Once again, you've fallen for one of my classic pranks.

12
00:00:43,780 --> 00:00:44,850
Bazinga!

13
00:00:50,480 --> 00:00:52,610
Now here's a peculiar e-mail.

14
00:00:52,680 --> 00:00:54,980
The president of the university wants me to meet him

15
00:00:55,050 --> 00:00:57,110
at his office tomorrow morning at 8:00 a.m.

16
00:00:57,180 --> 00:00:58,110
- Why? - It doesn't say.

17
00:00:58,180 --> 00:00:59,950
It must be an emergency.

18
00:01:00,010 --> 00:01:02,650
Everyone at the university knows I eat breakfast at 8:00

19
00:01:02,710 --> 00:01:05,210
and move my bowels at 8:20.

20
00:01:05,280 --> 00:01:09,410
Yes, how did we live before Twitter?

21
00:01:11,080 --> 00:01:12,880
I guess you'll find out what it is in the morning.

22
00:01:12,950 --> 00:01:15,710
That's 14 hours away.

23
00:01:15,780 --> 00:01:17,110
For the next 840 minutes,

24
00:01:17,180 --> 00:01:19,550
I'm effectively one of Heisenberg's particles;

25
00:01:19,610 --> 00:01:22,050
I know where I am or I know how fast I'm going,

26
00:01:22,110 --> 00:01:23,210
but I can't know both.

27
00:01:23,280 --> 00:01:24,950
Yet how am I supposed to carry on

28
00:01:25,010 --> 00:01:27,980
with this huge annoying thing hovering over my head?

29
00:01:28,050 --> 00:01:30,880
Yeah, I know the feeling.

30
00:01:56,140 --> 00:01:58,310
Leonard? Leonard?

31
00:01:58,380 --> 00:02:00,080
Leonard?

32
00:02:01,240 --> 00:02:02,910
Sheldon, it's 2:00 in the morning.

33
00:02:02,980 --> 00:02:06,480
Why is everybody keep telling me what time it is?

34
00:02:06,540 --> 00:02:07,580
Everybody?

35
00:02:07,640 --> 00:02:10,610
You, the president of the university, his wife,

36
00:02:10,680 --> 00:02:12,440
their sullen teenage daughter.

37
00:02:12,510 --> 00:02:17,910
That entire family is fascinated by what time it is and whether people know it.

38
00:02:17,980 --> 00:02:21,180
You went to President Seibert's house in the middle of the night?

39
00:02:21,240 --> 00:02:23,010
He didn't respond to my e-mail,

40
00:02:23,080 --> 00:02:24,240
his phone number is unlisted.

41
00:02:24,310 --> 00:02:26,880
Tell me what my other option was.

42
00:02:26,940 --> 00:02:29,280
You could have waited until morning.

43
00:02:29,340 --> 00:02:31,780
I know, look who I'm talking to.

44
00:02:32,880 --> 00:02:35,580
Do you remember the grant proposal I submitted

45
00:02:35,640 --> 00:02:38,410
to the National Science Foundation to detect slow-moving monopoles

46
00:02:38,480 --> 00:02:40,210
at the magnetic North Pole?

47
00:02:40,280 --> 00:02:44,080
Hardly a day goes by when I don't think about it.

48
00:02:44,140 --> 00:02:45,780
Aw, how nice.

49
00:02:45,840 --> 00:02:48,080
Well, a space opened up

50
00:02:48,140 --> 00:02:51,780
at the last minute on the NSF expedition to the Arctic Circle.

51
00:02:51,840 --> 00:02:54,480
Wait a minute.

52
00:02:55,910 --> 00:02:58,140
He offered to send you to the North Pole?

53
00:02:58,210 --> 00:02:59,310
Yes.

54
00:02:59,380 --> 00:03:01,440
In fact, he was quite enthusiastic.

55
00:03:01,510 --> 00:03:02,740
He said, "Frankly,

56
00:03:02,810 --> 00:03:05,410
if I could send you tonight, I would."

57
00:03:05,480 --> 00:03:08,710
Okay, well, do you want to go?

58
00:03:08,780 --> 00:03:10,780
Of course not.

59
00:03:10,840 --> 00:03:12,210
I'm a theoretical physicist.

60
00:03:12,280 --> 00:03:15,510
A career I chose in no small part because it's indoors,

61
00:03:15,580 --> 00:03:20,440
but if I'm able to detect slow-moving magnetic monopoles there,

62
00:03:20,510 --> 00:03:23,210
I will be the scientis to confirm string theory.

63
00:03:23,280 --> 00:03:25,280
People will write books about me.

64
00:03:25,340 --> 00:03:30,740
Third-graders will create macaroni-art dioramas depicting scenes from my life.

65
00:03:30,810 --> 00:03:35,410
Sure, maybe a tableau of me trying to pummel you to death.

66
00:03:35,480 --> 00:03:37,080
Sarcasm?

67
00:03:37,140 --> 00:03:38,780
Maybe.

68
00:03:40,780 --> 00:03:42,980
I'm on the horns of a dilemma.

69
00:03:43,040 --> 00:03:46,910
Can you imagine me, Sheldon Cooper, at the North Pole?

70
00:03:46,980 --> 00:03:49,010
Easy peasy, I'm doing it right now.

71
00:03:49,080 --> 00:03:52,240
I'm not good with cold, Leonard.

72
00:03:52,310 --> 00:03:54,380
How often have we had to leave a movie theater

73
00:03:54,440 --> 00:03:55,540
because I got a headache

74
00:03:55,610 --> 00:03:57,240
from drinking the Icee too fast?

75
00:03:57,310 --> 00:03:59,340
I can't go.

76
00:03:59,440 --> 00:04:00,540
Well, then don't go.

77
00:04:00,610 --> 00:04:03,010
How can you say that?

78
00:04:03,080 --> 00:04:05,140
The scientific opportunity of a lifetime

79
00:04:05,210 --> 00:04:07,480
presents itself and my best friend says "Don't go."

80
00:04:07,540 --> 00:04:08,710
All right, then go.

81
00:04:08,780 --> 00:04:12,080
Listen to you.

82
00:04:12,140 --> 00:04:14,440
How can I possibly go?

83
00:04:14,510 --> 00:04:16,910
Sheldon, what are the words I can say right now

84
00:04:16,980 --> 00:04:19,540
to end this conversation and let me go back to sleep?

85
00:04:19,610 --> 00:04:25,610
Odd, President Seibert posed the exact same question.

86
00:04:25,680 --> 00:04:27,610
How was it resolved?

87
00:04:27,680 --> 00:04:32,310
It wasn't. His wife set their dogs on me and rendered the question moot.

88
00:04:34,030 --> 00:04:36,760
Just imagine. If he says yes,

89
00:04:36,830 --> 00:04:41,200
we'll have an entire summer without Sheldon.

90
00:04:41,260 --> 00:04:43,760
We could play outside.

91
00:04:44,900 --> 00:04:47,530
We could sit on the left side of the couch.

92
00:04:47,600 --> 00:04:50,430
I could use the bathroom at 8:20.

93
00:04:54,600 --> 00:04:57,060
Our dreams are very small, aren't they?

94
00:04:58,930 --> 00:05:02,560
Good news, gentlemen, I have tentatively accepted...

95
00:05:02,630 --> 00:05:04,130
Yeah!

96
00:05:08,460 --> 00:05:11,660
...the invitation to join the Arctic Expedition.

97
00:05:11,730 --> 00:05:14,200
- It's not gonna be the same without you. - Godspeed.

98
00:05:14,970 --> 00:05:19,070
Thank you, but your sentiments may be premature.

99
00:05:19,140 --> 00:05:21,670
Ooh, I don't like where this is going.

100
00:05:21,740 --> 00:05:24,540
I would like to propose...

101
00:05:24,610 --> 00:05:26,210
that the three of you accompany me.

102
00:05:27,140 --> 00:05:29,570
To the North Pole?

103
00:05:29,640 --> 00:05:30,870
Yes.

104
00:05:30,940 --> 00:05:34,210
Is this just so we won't touch your stuff while you're away?

105
00:05:34,270 --> 00:05:37,910
I'll admit that was a concern.

106
00:05:37,970 --> 00:05:39,040
But the fact is,

107
00:05:39,110 --> 00:05:40,710
I'll need a support team.

108
00:05:40,770 --> 00:05:41,970
And the three of you

109
00:05:42,040 --> 00:05:43,040
are my first choice.

110
00:05:43,110 --> 00:05:43,790
Really?

111
00:05:43,970 --> 00:05:45,840
Well, there are others who might be more qualified,

112
00:05:45,900 --> 00:05:48,100
but the thought of interviewing them gave me a stomachache.

113
00:05:48,170 --> 00:05:51,840
Now, I know I'm proposing an enormous undertaking,

114
00:05:51,900 --> 00:05:54,640
so why don't you take a few moments to discuss it?

115
00:05:56,870 --> 00:05:58,740
We're not really gonna go to the North Pole

116
00:05:58,800 --> 00:05:59,940
with him, are we?

117
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:03,100
I'm still within earshot!

118
00:06:03,170 --> 00:06:05,700
You may want to wait for my door to close.

119
00:06:07,300 --> 00:06:08,470
We're not really gonna go

120
00:06:08,540 --> 00:06:10,470
to the North Pole with him, are we?

121
00:06:10,540 --> 00:06:12,140
Hang on. Let's talk about it.

122
00:06:12,200 --> 00:06:13,670
This is a National Science Foundation expedition.

123
00:06:13,740 --> 00:06:16,700
I don't know how we can turn it down.

124
00:06:16,770 --> 00:06:20,800
Easy. Instead of saying, "No, we don't want to go on an NSF expedition,"

125
00:06:20,870 --> 00:06:22,040
say, "No, we don't want to spend

126
00:06:22,100 --> 00:06:24,800
"three months stuck in a cabin in the Arctic Circle

127
00:06:24,870 --> 00:06:26,940
with an anal nutbag."

128
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:31,500
But if we were part of the team that confirmed string theory,

129
00:06:31,570 --> 00:06:34,800
we could drink for free in any bar in any college town

130
00:06:34,870 --> 00:06:39,800
with a university that has a strong science program.

131
00:06:39,870 --> 00:06:41,200
Howard, this is big science.

132
00:06:41,270 --> 00:06:43,240
You could be the engineer who builds the equipment

133
00:06:43,300 --> 00:06:45,100
that puts us on the cover of magazines.

134
00:06:45,170 --> 00:06:47,300
I could also be the engineer

135
00:06:47,370 --> 00:06:50,370
who builds the crossbow that kills Sheldon.

136
00:06:53,700 --> 00:06:57,140
You still might get on a magazine.

137
00:06:57,200 --> 00:07:00,470
So you guys are seriously considering this?

138
00:07:00,540 --> 00:07:03,070
- Yes. - And you think you can put up with Sheldon?

139
00:07:03,140 --> 00:07:05,970
Well, I'm a Hindu.

140
00:07:06,040 --> 00:07:08,700
My religion teaches that if we suffer in this life

141
00:07:08,770 --> 00:07:10,400
we are rewarded in the next.

142
00:07:10,470 --> 00:07:12,640
Three months at the North Pole with Sheldon,

143
00:07:12,700 --> 00:07:16,470
and I'm reborn as a well-hung billionaire with wings!

144
00:07:22,300 --> 00:07:26,700
Well, gentlemen, have you reached a decision?

145
00:07:26,770 --> 00:07:28,240
I'm in.

146
00:07:28,300 --> 00:07:29,870
Me, too.

147
00:07:31,940 --> 00:07:33,840
Oh, damn it. Peer pressure. Fine.

148
00:07:35,150 --> 00:07:36,400
Excellent.

149
00:07:36,400 --> 00:07:37,500
Just an FYI,

150
00:07:37,570 --> 00:07:40,200
as I am the expedition's team leader,

151
00:07:40,270 --> 00:07:42,230
protocol dictates that be phrased, "Fine, sir."

152
00:07:42,300 --> 00:07:44,100
But don't worry,

153
00:07:44,170 --> 00:07:45,270
there will be a briefing.

154
00:07:53,850 --> 00:07:55,380
Penny.

155
00:07:55,450 --> 00:07:57,480
Sheldon.

156
00:08:03,580 --> 00:08:05,310
Penny.

157
00:08:05,410 --> 00:08:07,410
Sheldon.

158
00:08:08,480 --> 00:08:09,410
Penny.

159
00:08:09,480 --> 00:08:10,410
Sheldon.

160
00:08:10,480 --> 00:08:11,410
Penny.

161
00:08:11,480 --> 00:08:12,410
Sheldon.

162
00:08:12,480 --> 00:08:13,410
Penny!

163
00:08:13,480 --> 00:08:14,410
Sheldon!

164
00:08:14,480 --> 00:08:15,410
Penny!

165
00:08:16,410 --> 00:08:17,850
What do you want?

166
00:08:17,910 --> 00:08:20,650
I need access to the Cheesecake Factory's walk-in freezer.

167
00:08:20,650 --> 00:08:23,010
Now, honey, I already told you, the hamburger

168
00:08:23,010 --> 00:08:25,380
meat is fresh and stored at a safe temperature.

169
00:08:26,180 --> 00:08:31,780
No. This is to train for a three-month expedition to the magnetic North Pole.

170
00:08:31,850 --> 00:08:32,950
What?

171
00:08:33,010 --> 00:08:37,150
I don't know how that sentence could possibly confuse you...

172
00:08:38,610 --> 00:08:41,650
but to elaborate, I'm going to the Arctic Circle

173
00:08:41,710 --> 00:08:43,510
with Leonard, Wolowitz and Koothrappali.

174
00:08:43,580 --> 00:08:44,710
You're all going?

175
00:08:44,780 --> 00:08:46,350
- Yes. - For three months?

176
00:08:46,410 --> 00:08:47,050
Yes.

177
00:08:47,110 --> 00:08:48,510
Excuse me.

178
00:08:51,550 --> 00:08:53,950
Is that a yes or a no on the freezer?

179
00:08:54,010 --> 00:08:57,180
The woman has the attention span of a gnat.

180
00:08:57,250 --> 00:08:58,980
- Hey, Leonard. - Hey.

181
00:08:59,050 --> 00:09:01,050
Sheldon says you're going to the North Pole.

182
00:09:01,110 --> 00:09:02,580
Yeah. Pretty cool, huh?

183
00:09:02,650 --> 00:09:05,650
Yeah. I'm just a little surprised you didn't tell me.

184
00:09:05,710 --> 00:09:07,850
Oh, well, it all happened kind of fast.

185
00:09:07,910 --> 00:09:10,550
And we had to get physicals and buy thermal underwear

186
00:09:10,610 --> 00:09:13,550
and study up on, you know, snow and stuff.

187
00:09:14,650 --> 00:09:16,580
Sorry, I was gonna tell you.

188
00:09:16,650 --> 00:09:18,580
Oh, hey, no, you don't have to apologize.

189
00:09:18,650 --> 00:09:20,310
There's no reason you have to tell me.

190
00:09:20,380 --> 00:09:22,050
I was just... you know, surprised.

191
00:09:22,110 --> 00:09:23,480
Yes, yes, you were busy,

192
00:09:23,550 --> 00:09:24,750
you were surprised, all very fascinating.

193
00:09:24,810 --> 00:09:26,550
Now where do we stand on the freezer?

194
00:09:27,480 --> 00:09:28,850
Is he serious?

195
00:09:28,910 --> 00:09:30,710
Actually, it would help.

196
00:09:30,780 --> 00:09:33,480
All right, I'll see what I can do.

197
00:09:33,550 --> 00:09:37,010
So, three months at the North Pole.

198
00:09:37,080 --> 00:09:38,610
Wow, that is...

199
00:09:38,680 --> 00:09:39,350
awesome.

200
00:09:39,410 --> 00:09:40,480
Yeah.

201
00:09:40,550 --> 00:09:42,780
I'm sorry, but at what point do you put this

202
00:09:42,850 --> 00:09:45,650
"see what you can do" plan into action?

203
00:09:45,910 --> 00:09:48,880
Just a warning, Sheldon.

204
00:09:48,880 --> 00:09:51,080
The freezer locks from the outside.

205
00:09:56,510 --> 00:09:59,180
- She seems upset to you? - No.

206
00:09:59,250 --> 00:10:00,880
- Did she seem upset to you? - No.

207
00:10:00,950 --> 00:10:02,780
Oh, good, I got it right.

208
00:10:05,810 --> 00:10:07,850
Are you upset?

209
00:10:07,850 --> 00:10:08,970
A little bit.

210
00:10:09,680 --> 00:10:13,780
Two for two. I'm on fire.

211
00:10:13,850 --> 00:10:16,310
I mean, I know she's not my girlfriend or anything,

212
00:10:16,380 --> 00:10:18,350
but wouldn't you think she'd feel a little bad

213
00:10:18,410 --> 00:10:20,450
that I'm going to be gone for the whole summer?

214
00:10:20,510 --> 00:10:23,450
That feels like a bonus question.

215
00:10:23,510 --> 00:10:26,740
I'm gonna stop here while I'm ahead but I've had a great time.

216
00:10:38,700 --> 00:10:42,640
All right, the purpose of this drill

217
00:10:42,690 --> 00:10:45,720
is to acclimate us to the use of tools in extreme temperatures

218
00:10:45,790 --> 00:10:47,690
such as we will face in the Arctic Circle.

219
00:10:47,760 --> 00:10:49,270
Where are your tools?

220
00:10:49,340 --> 00:10:51,900
Right here.

221
00:10:51,960 --> 00:10:55,280
All right, team, open up your practice kits.

222
00:10:55,350 --> 00:10:57,090
As the university did not permit me

223
00:10:57,150 --> 00:10:58,700
to bring the actual equipment we'll be using

224
00:10:58,760 --> 00:11:01,130
to the Cheesecake Factory, because apparently,

225
00:11:01,200 --> 00:11:03,340
I'm "ridiculous,"

226
00:11:03,390 --> 00:11:05,170
I've provided substitutes

227
00:11:05,240 --> 00:11:07,040
which will exercise your fine motor skills.

228
00:11:07,110 --> 00:11:11,320
Leonard, you will be doing a series of complex mathematical problems

229
00:11:11,380 --> 00:11:15,090
on a vintage Casio model 1175 calculator watch I received

230
00:11:15,160 --> 00:11:18,570
when I won the Earth Science medal in 3rd grade.

231
00:11:19,430 --> 00:11:22,160
Treat it with respect.

232
00:11:22,220 --> 00:11:27,150
Raj, you will be painting sideburns and a Van Dyke

233
00:11:27,150 --> 00:11:29,880
on a six-inch figurine of Legolas the elf.

234
00:11:30,290 --> 00:11:31,570
Now, remember,

235
00:11:31,640 --> 00:11:34,500
a Van Dyke is a goatee without a mustache.

236
00:11:35,350 --> 00:11:37,880
Wolowitz, you will be completing

237
00:11:37,950 --> 00:11:40,250
a series of delicate surgical procedures

238
00:11:40,310 --> 00:11:42,940
on the classic children's game "Operation."

239
00:11:46,490 --> 00:11:49,310
To begin with, you will

240
00:11:49,380 --> 00:11:53,190
"remove funny bone for two hundred dollars."

241
00:11:53,260 --> 00:11:56,580
For this I went to MIT.

242
00:11:57,180 --> 00:11:58,430
And begin.

243
00:12:02,360 --> 00:12:05,250
I think I swallowed some paint!

244
00:12:06,310 --> 00:12:08,850
I can't press any of the buttons with my gloves.

245
00:12:10,650 --> 00:12:13,340
Oh, son of a bitch!

246
00:12:13,410 --> 00:12:14,920
Adversity is to be expected.

247
00:12:14,990 --> 00:12:16,100
Continue.

248
00:12:16,170 --> 00:12:19,550
Oh, boy, am I gonna get sued.

249
00:12:19,620 --> 00:12:21,560
Okay, I can't do this.

250
00:12:21,620 --> 00:12:22,680
Me either.

251
00:12:22,750 --> 00:12:24,320
Gentlemen, use your imagination.

252
00:12:24,380 --> 00:12:25,470
Innovate.

253
00:12:25,540 --> 00:12:28,130
Did Han Solo let Luke Skywalker

254
00:12:28,130 --> 00:12:29,940
freeze to death on the ice planet of Hoth?

255
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:31,220
No.

256
00:12:31,280 --> 00:12:33,100
He cut open a tauntaun

257
00:12:33,160 --> 00:12:35,360
and used its internal body heat to warm him up.

258
00:12:35,420 --> 00:12:36,870
You heard the man.

259
00:12:36,940 --> 00:12:38,480
Hold him down and I'll cut him open.

260
00:12:38,550 --> 00:12:42,030
Hang on, I know I don't possess the tools of leadership,

261
00:12:42,100 --> 00:12:44,140
but I don't understand why we can't assemble the equipment

262
00:12:44,200 --> 00:12:47,010
inside the hut and then take it outside.

263
00:12:48,530 --> 00:12:50,180
I hadn't thought of that.

264
00:12:51,990 --> 00:12:54,300
I guess we're done here.

265
00:12:59,180 --> 00:13:02,700
Here, drink slash eat this.

266
00:13:02,750 --> 00:13:04,170
What is it?

267
00:13:04,230 --> 00:13:06,400
Hot chocolate with a stick of butter.

268
00:13:06,470 --> 00:13:09,820
Okay, why?

269
00:13:09,890 --> 00:13:11,860
Because in the frigid temperatures in the Arctic,

270
00:13:11,920 --> 00:13:14,030
we need to consume at least 5,000 calories a day

271
00:13:14,090 --> 00:13:15,610
just to maintain our body weight.

272
00:13:15,670 --> 00:13:17,240
Sheldon, you know I can't eat butter.

273
00:13:17,310 --> 00:13:18,400
I'm lactose intolerant.

274
00:13:18,470 --> 00:13:19,750
Way ahead of you, that's an

275
00:13:19,810 --> 00:13:21,230
"I Can't Believe It's Not Butter" stick.

276
00:13:22,490 --> 00:13:25,030
But mammy, all the other guys are going to the North Pole.

277
00:13:25,100 --> 00:13:27,340
I don't care what the other guys are doing.

278
00:13:27,400 --> 00:13:31,190
If the other guys jumped in the Bay of Bengal and tried to swim to Sri Lanka,

279
00:13:31,190 --> 00:13:32,170
would you follow them?

280
00:13:32,230 --> 00:13:35,280
If you were standing behind me nagging, I might.

281
00:13:35,750 --> 00:13:37,530
Don't talk back to your mother.

282
00:13:37,600 --> 00:13:40,190
This trip is much too dangerous, Rajesh.

283
00:13:40,190 --> 00:13:40,990
No, it's not.

284
00:13:40,990 --> 00:13:42,110
Howard, tell them.

285
00:13:43,180 --> 00:13:46,790
Doctor and Mrs. Koothrappali, namaste.

286
00:13:47,970 --> 00:13:49,190
I understand your concern,

287
00:13:49,250 --> 00:13:52,210
but if it'll make you feel any better, my mother is fine with me going,

288
00:13:52,280 --> 00:13:57,380
and this is a woman who kept a safety rail on my bed until I was 17.

289
00:13:57,430 --> 00:14:01,220
So, she has no problem with her son being eaten by a walrus?

290
00:14:01,280 --> 00:14:03,020
That's very unlikely, Mrs. Koothrappali.

291
00:14:03,120 --> 00:14:04,330
If Raj dies,

292
00:14:04,400 --> 00:14:07,260
it'll be from frostbite, gangrene, acute sunburn

293
00:14:07,330 --> 00:14:11,200
or being ripped to shreds by a 1,500 pound polar bear.

294
00:14:11,270 --> 00:14:15,160
Ma, I'm putting you on speakerphone with Raj's parents.

295
00:14:15,160 --> 00:14:18,200
Can you tell them that you're okay with me going to the Arctic?

296
00:14:18,270 --> 00:14:19,420
Arctic?!

297
00:14:19,480 --> 00:14:23,230
I thought you said Arkansas!

298
00:14:23,300 --> 00:14:24,880
I didn't say that.

299
00:14:24,940 --> 00:14:26,260
You never listen to me!

300
00:14:26,320 --> 00:14:28,330
He doesn't tell me anything!

301
00:14:28,390 --> 00:14:32,470
He lives a secret life because he's ashamed!

302
00:14:33,430 --> 00:14:35,730
Hey, Leonard, can I talk to you for a sec?

303
00:14:35,790 --> 00:14:40,490
Sure, but let's go out here where there's a little less yelling and guilt.

304
00:14:40,560 --> 00:14:42,300
What's up?

305
00:14:42,370 --> 00:14:44,760
Well, I got you a little going away present.

306
00:14:44,830 --> 00:14:46,570
Oh, a blanket.

307
00:14:46,630 --> 00:14:48,080
Oh, no, no, no, not just a blanket.

308
00:14:48,150 --> 00:14:51,960
See, it has sleeves.

309
00:14:56,560 --> 00:14:59,980
So, you can, you know, be all snoodled up

310
00:15:00,040 --> 00:15:01,320
while you do your science stuff.

311
00:15:01,390 --> 00:15:05,200
Oh, wow, cool.

312
00:15:05,260 --> 00:15:07,600
Oh, I'm gonna miss you.

313
00:15:15,360 --> 00:15:17,190
- See you later. - Bye.

314
00:15:22,570 --> 00:15:24,340
I told you no!

315
00:15:24,400 --> 00:15:25,780
Why don't you believe me?

316
00:15:25,850 --> 00:15:28,640
'Cause it doesn't make sense to me!

317
00:15:28,710 --> 00:15:31,670
How can it be that in the entire country of India,

318
00:15:31,730 --> 00:15:34,200
there isn't one Outback Steakhouse?!

319
00:15:42,180 --> 00:15:44,350
Sheldon... Sheldon?

320
00:15:44,410 --> 00:15:46,720
I want a cookie, Meemaw.

321
00:15:49,310 --> 00:15:50,390
Sheldon, it's me.

322
00:15:50,460 --> 00:15:52,930
But Meemaw just made cookies.

323
00:15:53,690 --> 00:15:57,600
Listen, I don't know if I can go on the expedition.

324
00:15:58,650 --> 00:16:00,060
What?

325
00:16:00,130 --> 00:16:01,840
I don't think I can go to the North Pole.

326
00:16:01,910 --> 00:16:03,640
Okay, Leonard,

327
00:16:03,710 --> 00:16:06,640
I know you're concerned about disappointing me

328
00:16:06,700 --> 00:16:09,230
but I want you to take comfort from the knowledge

329
00:16:09,300 --> 00:16:12,680
that my expectations of you are very low.

330
00:16:12,750 --> 00:16:15,670
Yeah, that's very comforting.

331
00:16:15,740 --> 00:16:17,540
Comforting is a part of leadership.

332
00:16:17,610 --> 00:16:20,690
It's not a part I care for, but such is my burden.

333
00:16:20,760 --> 00:16:24,510
Terrific, it's just that I don't think Penny wants me to go.

334
00:16:24,580 --> 00:16:28,750
Assuming that's a valid reason not to go, which it isn't,

335
00:16:28,820 --> 00:16:31,970
how do you know this? Did she say it?

336
00:16:31,970 --> 00:16:33,780
Not exactly.

337
00:16:33,780 --> 00:16:36,660
But she said she was gonna miss me and she gave me this.

338
00:16:39,020 --> 00:16:40,960
What is it?

339
00:16:41,030 --> 00:16:42,340
It's a blanket with sleeves.

340
00:16:42,410 --> 00:16:45,430
Oh, that's clever.

341
00:16:47,440 --> 00:16:49,080
Let me see if I understand this correctly.

342
00:16:49,150 --> 00:16:54,370
Her missing you is an emotional state you find desirable?

343
00:16:54,370 --> 00:16:56,610
- Yes, obviously. - All right.

344
00:16:56,680 --> 00:17:00,460
Well, given that missing you is predicated on you leaving,

345
00:17:00,530 --> 00:17:04,000
logic dictates you must leave.

346
00:17:04,070 --> 00:17:08,270
Yes, okay, but I'm gonna be gone for three months.

347
00:17:08,340 --> 00:17:11,330
What if she doesn't miss me that long and she meets someone else?

348
00:17:11,400 --> 00:17:15,310
She does have a short attention span.

349
00:17:15,370 --> 00:17:16,550
So, I can't go.

350
00:17:16,620 --> 00:17:18,790
Leonard, you may be right.

351
00:17:18,860 --> 00:17:20,630
It appears that Penny secretly wants you

352
00:17:20,690 --> 00:17:23,790
in her life in a very intimate and carnal fashion.

353
00:17:23,850 --> 00:17:25,920
You really think so?

354
00:17:25,990 --> 00:17:27,660
Of course not.

355
00:17:30,160 --> 00:17:32,130
Even in my sleep-deprived state, I've managed to

356
00:17:32,200 --> 00:17:35,350
pull off another one of my classic pranks.

357
00:17:35,420 --> 00:17:38,700
Bazinga!

358
00:17:45,280 --> 00:17:47,280
Oh, Leonard, what time is it?

359
00:17:47,350 --> 00:17:49,000
It's 7:00 a.m. I'm sorry it's early,

360
00:17:49,060 --> 00:17:51,230
but we're leaving soon, and I needed to talk to you.

361
00:17:51,290 --> 00:17:52,770
Okay.

362
00:17:52,840 --> 00:17:56,390
What did you mean when you said you're going to miss me?

363
00:17:56,450 --> 00:17:59,510
Um, I don't know.

364
00:17:59,570 --> 00:18:03,020
You'll be gone and I'll notice.

365
00:18:03,090 --> 00:18:06,940
Okay, well, um, what about this?

366
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:08,320
What does this mean?

367
00:18:08,380 --> 00:18:13,870
Wine, credit card and late night television are a bad combination.

368
00:18:14,880 --> 00:18:16,530
All right, fine.

369
00:18:16,600 --> 00:18:18,470
What about that really long hug?

370
00:18:18,530 --> 00:18:19,610
What did that mean?

371
00:18:19,680 --> 00:18:22,080
That wasn't a long hug.

372
00:18:22,150 --> 00:18:26,120
It was at least five Mississippis.

373
00:18:26,190 --> 00:18:31,580
A standard hug is two Mississippis tops.

374
00:18:31,640 --> 00:18:34,570
Leonard, I don't know what to tell you.

375
00:18:34,640 --> 00:18:35,950
It was just a hug.

376
00:18:36,020 --> 00:18:38,090
Glad we cleared that up.

377
00:18:38,140 --> 00:18:39,620
Yeah.

378
00:18:39,690 --> 00:18:40,480
I guess I'll see you.

379
00:18:40,550 --> 00:18:42,680
Okay, have a safe trip.

380
00:18:42,750 --> 00:18:44,690
Thank you.

381
00:18:44,750 --> 00:18:45,800
- Bye. - Okay, bye.

382
00:18:51,690 --> 00:18:54,390
It means I wish you weren't going.

383
00:19:04,450 --> 00:19:05,930
Damn it.

384
00:19:05,990 --> 00:19:07,040
What?

385
00:19:07,110 --> 00:19:10,120
We're out of ice.

386
00:19:10,190 --> 00:19:12,360
All right, men, we begin initial assembly

387
00:19:12,430 --> 00:19:14,010
and deployment of the testing equipment

388
00:19:14,070 --> 00:19:16,530
starting tomorrow at 0700 hours,

389
00:19:16,600 --> 00:19:19,070
but until then, you are all off duty.

390
00:19:19,130 --> 00:19:22,150
I suggest you keep the shenanigans to a minimum

391
00:19:22,220 --> 00:19:25,440
as medical help is 18 hours away by dogsled.

392
00:19:25,500 --> 00:19:27,370
What are you working on?

393
00:19:27,440 --> 00:19:29,420
Crossbow.

394
00:19:31,390 --> 00:19:33,620
Hey, guys, can I just say something?

395
00:19:33,680 --> 00:19:35,130
How about we take a moment to think about

396
00:19:35,200 --> 00:19:36,580
where we are right now?

397
00:19:36,640 --> 00:19:39,740
This is literally the top of the world.

398
00:19:39,790 --> 00:19:42,300
Only a handful of people in all of human history

399
00:19:42,300 --> 00:19:45,740
will ever see what we are going to see.

400
00:19:45,740 --> 00:19:47,180
He's right.

401
00:19:47,180 --> 00:19:48,180
Yeah, wow.

402
00:19:48,190 --> 00:19:50,760
It is remarkable.

403
00:19:50,820 --> 00:19:53,320
So, who's up for a movie?

404
00:19:54,270 --> 00:19:55,680
Good idea, what do you think?

405
00:19:55,750 --> 00:19:59,130
Ice Station Zebra or John Carpenter's The Thing?

406
00:19:59,200 --> 00:20:00,320
I say double feature.

407
00:20:00,390 --> 00:20:02,060
Dinner's ready!

408
00:20:02,120 --> 00:20:03,770
What are we having?

409
00:20:03,840 --> 00:20:06,300
Reconstituted Thai food.

410
00:20:06,360 --> 00:20:09,650
- Did you bring the dehydrated low-sodium soy sauce? - Check.

411
00:20:09,710 --> 00:20:11,360
- Freeze-dried spicy mustard? - Check.

412
00:20:11,430 --> 00:20:13,130
Flash-frozen brown rice, not white?

413
00:20:13,200 --> 00:20:17,000
Uh, oh, sorry.

414
00:20:17,070 --> 00:20:18,380
Not to worry.

415
00:20:18,450 --> 00:20:20,820
I hid it.

416
00:20:21,680 --> 00:20:23,970
Bazinga!

417
00:20:27,520 --> 00:20:29,820
You're in my spot.

418
00:20:37,740 --> 00:20:41,980
There's no time for a crossbow. Find me an icicle.

419
00:20:45,390 --> 00:20:49,080
Three months. This is gonna be great!

